Sometimes you can know that your own thinking or perception is becoming unreliable. The broad psychiatric term is insight—awareness that your experiences, beliefs, or behavior may be symptoms rather than straightforward reality.

It’s a spectrum: good/preserved insight (“this feels absolutely real, but I know my mind may be producing it”), partial insight (“I suspect something is off, but I’m not sure”), and poor/absent insight, sometimes called anosognosia in certain conditions, where the person genuinely cannot recognize the impairment.

For psychosis specifically, people sometimes casually say “psychosis with preserved insight” or “partial insight.” There’s also an older concept called double bookkeeping, where someone can hold an unusual internal reality while simultaneously functioning according to ordinary shared reality.

So the cliché “crazy people don’t know they’re crazy” is false. Self-monitoring can survive even when another part of cognition is misfiring. In Pantheon terms, the renderer can malfunction while the observer still notices a delta between its rendering and the shared-world anchors.